        {"h2_html": "What Clio <em>does well</em>",
         "body_html": '<p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch">Clio is a well-known legal practice management platform, and firms rely on it for good reason. Its own materials describe a single place to run the day-to-day work of a law firm: opening and organizing matters, tracking calendars and deadlines, logging billable time, generating invoices, storing case documents, and taking in new client details through intake forms. It is used across solo practices, growing firms, and larger legal teams, by the attorneys, paralegals, and staff who work in those files every day. If your bottleneck is keeping open matters organized and getting bills out the door, that is the kind of work Clio is built for.</p><p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch;margin-top:1rem">That is a real strength, and it helps to be precise about what it means. Practice management software is a tool your own team signs into and operates, and it keeps the work you already have organized and billed. That is a different thing from a service that answers your new-client call at nine at night, or a team that keeps your website, reviews, and lead follow-up running for you while the lawyers practice law. Both matter. They are just not the same job, and most firms feel the second gap long before the software can close it.</p>'},
